Some denominations have [[List of religious texts#Christianity|additional canonical texts]] beyond the Bible, including the [[Standard Works]] of the [[Latter Day Saints movement]] and ''[[Divine Principle]]'' in the [[Unification Church]].

Nearly all modern English translations of the Old Testament are based on a single manuscript, the [[Leningrad Codex]], copied in 1008 or 1009. It is a complete example of the Masoretic Text, and its published edition is used by the majority of scholars. The [[Aleppo Codex]] is the basis of the Hebrew University Bible Project in Jerusalem.{{sfn|VanderKam|Flint|2013|p=87}}

Since the Reformation era, [[Bible translations]] have been made into the common vernacular of many languages. The Bible continues to be translated to new languages, largely by Christian organizations such as [[Wycliffe Bible Translators]], [[New Tribes Mission]] and [[Bible society|Bible societies]]. [[Lamin Sanneh]] writes that tracing the impact on the local cultures of translating the Bible into local vernacular language shows it has produced "the movements of indigenization and cultural liberation".{{sfn|Sanneh|McClymond|2016|p=265}} "The translated scripture ... has become the benchmark of awakening and renewal".{{sfn|Sanneh|McClymond|2016|p=279}}

